# HTTP, Communication Models, Status Codes and Proxies

## Question 1: HTTP Versions and Why HTTP/3 Uses UDP

### A) Key Differences Between HTTP Versions

```mermaid
timeline
    title Evolution of HTTP
    HTTP/0.9 : Only GET : HTML transfer
    HTTP/1.0 : Headers : Status codes : Content types
    HTTP/1.1 : Persistent connections : Better caching
    HTTP/2 : Multiplexing : Header compression
    HTTP/3 : QUIC over UDP : Faster connections
```

| Version  | Key Features |
| -------- | ------------ |
| HTTP/0.9 | The first and simplest version. It only supported `GET` requests and was used to transfer HTML pages. |
| HTTP/1.0 | Added request and response headers, status codes such as `404`, and support for different content types. A new connection was usually created for each request. |
| HTTP/1.1 | Introduced persistent connections (`Keep-Alive`) and improved caching and request handling. |
| HTTP/2   | Handles multiple requests at the same time over one connection. Also compresses headers and reduces communication overhead. |
| HTTP/3   | Uses the `QUIC` protocol over `UDP`. Reduces connection delays and improves performance on unstable networks. |

### B) Why Does HTTP/3 Use UDP?

`HTTP/3` uses `UDP` because it has lower overhead and allows a faster transport protocol called `QUIC` to be built on top of it. `UDP` does not provide reliable delivery or congestion control by itself. These features are provided by `QUIC`.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph H2["HTTP/2 stack"]
        direction TB
        A1["HTTP/2"] --> A2["TLS"] --> A3["TCP"] --> A4["IP"]
    end
    subgraph H3["HTTP/3 stack"]
        direction TB
        B1["HTTP/3"] --> B2["QUIC<br/>reliability + congestion control + encryption"] --> B3["UDP"] --> B4["IP"]
    end
```

One important advantage of `QUIC` is that losing a packet in one data stream does not necessarily stop the transfer of data in other streams. `QUIC` also provides built-in encryption and reduces the time needed to establish a secure connection. As a result, `HTTP/3` can load web pages faster, especially on unstable networks.

