## Question 5: Choose the Appropriate HTTP Status Code for Each REST API Scenario

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q{"Scenario"}
    Q --> A["Database problem, login fails"] --> A1["500 or 503"]
    Q --> B["Username does not exist"] --> B1["401"]
    Q --> C["Website moved to another domain"] --> C1["301 or 308"]
    Q --> D["Rate limit exceeded"] --> D1["429"]
    Q --> E["JWT refresh succeeded"] --> E1["200 or 204"]
    Q --> F["Blocked IP address"] --> F1["403"]
```

### 1. The Website Database Has a Problem, and Login Requests Fail

Suitable codes: `500 Internal Server Error` or `503 Service Unavailable`

If the website database has a problem and the server cannot process login requests, `500` may be used because an internal server error has occurred. However, if the database is temporarily unavailable and the login service cannot be used, `503` is more appropriate because it indicates that the service is temporarily unavailable.

### 2. The Entered Username Does Not Exist on the Website, and Login Fails

Suitable code: `401 Unauthorized`

When a user enters a username that does not exist, authentication fails, and the server can return `401`. This code indicates that the authentication credentials are invalid. It is better to show the same error message for an incorrect username and an incorrect password so that attackers cannot determine whether an account exists.

### 3. The Website Has Moved to Another Domain

Suitable codes: `301 Moved Permanently` or `308 Permanent Redirect`

If a website has permanently moved to another domain, the server can return `301` with the `Location` header to redirect the browser to the new address. The `308` code is also suitable for permanent redirects. The difference is that `308` preserves the HTTP request method. For example, if `old.com` moves to `new.com`, the server can include the new address in the `Location` header.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Browser
    participant O as old.com
    participant N as new.com
    B->>O: GET /
    O-->>B: 301 Moved Permanently, Location: https://new.com
    B->>N: GET /
    N-->>B: 200 OK
```

### 4. A User Sends More Requests Than the Allowed Limit

Suitable code: `429 Too Many Requests`

If a user sends more requests to an API than the allowed limit within a specific period, the server can return `429`. For example, if the API limit is 100 requests per minute and the user exceeds this limit, additional requests may be rejected. The server can also use the `Retry-After` header to tell the user when to try again.

### 5. A JWT Refresh Request Succeeds, and the New Token Is Returned in a Header

Suitable code: `200 OK`

If a user sends a request to refresh a `JWT` and the server validates the `Refresh Token` and generates a new access token, `200` is appropriate because the operation was successful. The new token can be included in a response header for the client to receive. If the response has no body, `204 No Content` may also be appropriate, provided that the new token is still available in a header.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant S as Server
    C->>S: Refresh request (Refresh Token)
    Note over S: Validates the Refresh Token
    S-->>C: 200 OK, new access token in a header
```

### 6. A User Tries to Access the Service from an IP Address Blocked Due to Sanctions

Suitable code: `403 Forbidden`

If a user sends a request from an IP address that is not allowed by the service's access policies, the server can return `403`. This code means that the server received the request but refuses to allow access. For example, if access from IP addresses in a specific region is blocked, requests from those addresses may be rejected with `403`.

