## Question 6: Identify the Proxy Type Used in Each Scenario

### 1. Using University Servers to Download Ubuntu Packages Instead of Central Servers

**Proxy type:** Caching Proxy

A university server stores copies of packages so users do not need to connect directly to the original servers every time they download the same packages. This reduces internet bandwidth usage, improves download speed, and reduces the load on central servers.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    U1["Student 1"] --> P["University caching proxy"]
    U2["Student 2"] --> P
    P -->|"First request only"| O["Ubuntu central servers"]
    P -.->|"Later requests served from cache"| U2
```

### 2. Distributing Requests Based on Their Geographic Origin

**Proxy type:** Reverse Proxy with Geo-routing

The proxy directs requests to suitable servers based on the geographic location of the user. For example, requests from users in Europe are sent to servers in Europe, while requests from users in Asia are sent to servers in Asia. This reduces latency and improves response times.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    E["User in Europe"] --> R["Reverse Proxy<br/>Geo-routing"]
    A["User in Asia"] --> R
    R --> SE["Europe servers"]
    R --> SA["Asia servers"]
```

### 3. Adding Authentication to a Very Old Service

**Proxy type:** Reverse Proxy

A `Reverse Proxy` is placed in front of the old service and performs authentication before forwarding requests to it. This makes it possible to add features such as token validation, username checks, and password authentication without changing the old service's code. Requests are forwarded to the original service only when the authentication information is valid.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    C["Client"] --> R{"Reverse Proxy<br/>Authentication"}
    R -->|"Valid credentials"| L["Old service"]
    R -->|"Invalid credentials"| X["Rejected"]
```


